>    There was a recent thread in sci.physics on QM interpretations, and
> a couple of people said that the Many-Worlds interpretation is the
> only interpretation consistent in a universe with gravity. These
> advocates of Many-Worlds came primarily from quantum gravity/cosmology
> research backgrounds. 

Okay, my background is sufficiently similar to see where they're coming
from.  Strictly speaking they are correct - many-worlds is the only
interpretation consistent with a universe with gravity.  But the same
is true of a universe without gravity.

This myth got started by Everett in his original many-worlds article 
[H Everett III, Rev. Physics, 29, 454 (1957)], who said 
     "[many-worlds] may therefore prove a fruitful framework for the
     quantization of general relativity."
The article was presented by Everett to a quantum gravity symposium,
which is why he probably said it - trying to make it seem particularly
relevant to his audience, I would guess.
Unfortunately this got misinterpreted by many reviewers to mean that
many-worlds only had relevance to quantum gravity, forgetting (or more
likely, never understanding) that Everett's work is applicable to non-
relativistic QM as well.  And since QG is not understood they believe
that when QG/TOE emerges it will supersede MW.

Everett's contribution was to demonstrate that the wavefunction collapse
and probability postulates of QM were unnecessary.  By removing the
artificial dichotomy between the observer and the observed Everett
showed that the usual, subjective experience of observers and collapsing
wavefunctions, now treated as reductionist automata, would arise as a
consequence of the maths of QM.  Once this point is grasped the
standard, Copenhagen interpretation is seen for what it is - requiring
one law for the observer and one law for the observed - totally
unscientific.  As an unscientific theory it has no place being applied
to any universe, whether gravity is involved or not.

For a theory to be judged scientific it has to submit to the dictates
of reductionism - that is the same laws of inanimate physics must be
applied to living and conscious beings.  Otherwise we are back in the
era of vitalism or alchemy.  The Copenhagen interpretation is some type
of throwback to these earlier dark times.  By jettisoning all the
metaphysical garbage from yestercentury we invariably arrive at a theory
where living entities exist in superpositions of different states (as,
say, electrons do), and we have many-worlds/many-minds, call it what you
will.

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

PS another reason is that QG researchers tend use many-worlds is because
when the universe as a whole is treated quantum-mechanically you can't
invoke external observers to collapse wavefunctions.  Never mind that
the whole concept of wavefunction collapse is illogical.  Thinking about
the whole universe seems to bring the illogicality to the forefront of
thought.
