From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Subject: QUANTUM: MMI vs. MWI

Of the various interpretations of quantum mechanics, the question
nowadays is not "Copenhagenism" vs. something else, but Many Minds
vs. Many Worlds.  MMI hardly seems to have been mentioned on the list.

Well, here is my interpretation.  I haven't read any detailed accounts
of MMI, but from what I can tell it sounds close to my version.  Perhaps
someone would like to comment on whether this is MMI, and either way,
what problems there are. 

The nature of reality:

(1) I have subjective mental experiences [firsthand empirical knowledge].

(2) Subjective mental experiences occur in the minds of other humans (and
those of other animals to a lesser degree) [from observation of behaviour,
and induction from (1) above -- why assume that I'm the Only One?].

(3) Reality is not restricted to mental events but also consists of an
external material world -- an objective reality which mental events 
relate to [axiom accepted by all but empiricist idealist monists] (also
poorly described as "existence exists", literally speaking a tautology).

(4) The Double-Slit Experiment.  All matter appears to have a dual
characteristic, exhibiting both wave-like and particle-like behaviour.
E.g., a beam of light fired through a single slit acts like a stream of
particles; however add an extra slit and it behaves like waves, then
watch which slit the "particle-wave" goes through, and it reverts to   
the actions of particles.  [Empirical knowledge from 20th century
experiments.]  So the entities "know" whether they have a choice of
slits to go through, and "know" whether they're being watched!  [This is
how some interpret it; however, the interpretation is so plumb crazy
that it is almost certain to be false.] 

(5) The "EPR Paradox" thought experiment.  Let two particles interact,
and measure the total momentum precisely.  Later on, measure particle A's
momentum precisely.  This instantly allows us to know the momentum of
particle B.  Since accurate knowledge of momentum has been shown to 
preclude accurate knowledge of position, this implies that the later
measurement of A suddenly "forces" B to take up a fuzzily defined position
and an accurately defined momentum.  (It could have been carried out the
other way round.)  "Ghostly action at a distance", a violation of
causality.  [Another crazy interpretation which can almost certainly be
beaten.] 

(6) The "Bell's Inequality" Experiments.  A modern variation on the "EPR
Paradox", involving measurements of photon polarisation.  "Bell's
Inequality" was shown to be violated precisely as quantum mechanics
predicted, in almost all the experiments.  This implied "ghostly action
at a distance".  [It's time for a new, expanded perspective.]

(7) Reality is not restricted to a single external material world, but
consists of all possible permutations of reality which are compatible
with a given observer's subjective mental states.  [Eliminating absurd
notions whilst providing an interpretation compatible with empirical
knowledge -- why assume that what one sees is the Only One True Reality
when this requires either the postulate of "ghostly action at a distance"
or that the universe is like some weird infinitely complex Newtonmas Tree?]

(8) The mind and stream of consciousness derives from the brain -- an
extremely complex structure in 3D space (or a very complicated set of
world-lines).  But the brain also takes on a structure (albeit nothing
like as complex) in "reality space".  "Reality space" consists of an
infinite or extremely large number of alternative worlds / parallel
universes / reality permutations.  An individual's brain, and hence mind,
occupies all of those worlds where its physical structure is either
exactly the same, or the difference is so slight that there is no
resultant differentiation of the consciousness streams.  Moreover, the
distribution of these minds across reality space, and relative abundance
and scarcity of alternative worlds, is determined according to a wave-
function as per Schrodinger's equations.  [The most philosophically
satisfying interpretation of (1) - (7) above.]

(9) "Particle-waves" are particles, and we're the waves.  [Conclusion.]

In the Everett Many Worlds interpretation, the universe splits whenever
there is a choice at the quantum level.  But why does it split, and why
does it split in exactly the way it does?  I prefer to think of all the
possible permutations of reality as being separate, self-contained 4D
units, where all the world-lines are very well defined and locked in
place.  An individual universe is deterministic; however an experimenter
with a five-dimensional mind will observe "quantum randomness", with the
possibility of chance electron transitions or radioactive decays occuring,
each of them a "rebel without a cause".

But what about those animals where their brains are not distributed
across reality space in the correct manner?  They would still be in a
super-position, but fail to form the correct wave function.  Well, it's
likely that the actual distribution of possible permutations of worlds
works out so as to yield the wave function, and you can keep diverging
and maintain the wave function.  But if a species' brains were distributed
incorrectly, they would be at several disadvantages, and would probably
be outcompeted.  For instance, being colour blind, they would be less
capable of identifying prey and predators.  And their solid-state
semiconductor electronics industry would be rather hit, if they got that
far.

Finally we have the question of the number of possible universes.  If
not infinite, it's certainly extremely high.  But if it's not infinite,
there is the risk of running out of universes if divergence of mind
exceeds convergence.  If things suddenly turn into monochrome, and you
hear a voice, "Welcome Sir, may I be of assistance?" -- look for a
spaceship to steal, sharpish.  You could be at the 'Restaurant at The
End of The Universe!'

